{
 "title": "Type Theory and Functional Programming",
 "byline": "Simon Thompson \u00b7 Addison-Wesley, 1991; author\u2019s free edition, 1999",
 "note": "PDF page = printed page + 13. Bibliography and rule tables are not broken down. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "An introduction to Martin-L\u00f6f\u2019s constructive type theory as both a logic and a programming language. After background on logic, the \u03bb-calculus and constructive mathematics, Thompson presents the theory through the Curry\u2013Howard correspondence: propositions as types and proofs as programs, with rules for connectives, quantifiers, natural numbers, trees and equality. He then explores its metatheory (normalization, equality, universes, well-founded types), applies it to program development and verification (quicksort, vectors, the Polish flag, program transformation), and surveys extensions such as subset and quotient types, inductive and coinductive types and partial objects, ending with foundations and related work.",
